---
import LessonLayout from '../../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
const lessonRef = "CCC 1285-1321";
const lessonScripture = "Jn 20:22-23; Acts 8:14-17; 2 Cor 1:21-22";
---
<LessonLayout title="Confirmation" cic={lessonRef} scripture={lessonScripture}>

<section class="content">

<h2>Big Question</h2>

<p class="big-question">What does it mean to be "sealed" with the Holy Spirit?</p>

<p>
Picture a letter with a wax seal. Once sealed, it bears the mark of its sender, authentic and unbreakable. In the ancient world, a seal meant ownership, protection, and authenticity. In Confirmation, God seals us with the Holy Spirit. We are marked as his own, protected by his power, and confirmed in our faith. This is not a graduation from catechesis — it is the beginning of a new mission. The confirmed Christian is sent as a witness, strengthened to defend the faith, empowered to live as a true disciple of Christ.
</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>What is Confirmation?</h2>

<p>
Confirmation is the sacrament that completes Baptism and strengthens the gifts of the Holy Spirit received at Baptism. The Catechism calls it the "sacrament of Christian maturity" (CCC 1308). It is not that we are immature before — but we are now strengthened for adult witness.
</p>

<p>
The essential rite of Confirmation is the anointing with chrism (holy oil) on the forehead, accompanied by the laying on of hands and the words: "Be sealed with the gift of the Holy Spirit" (CCC 1300). The anointing signifies the gift of the Spirit, and the seal marks the Christian forever as belonging to Christ.
</p>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--semilla">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">Activity</h4>
<p>Think about a time you had to stand up for something you believed in. Did you feel ready? Confirmation prepares us to stand up for Christ with courage.</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>The Seven Gifts of the Holy Spirit</h2>

<p>
Confirmation increases the seven gifts of the Holy Spirit received in Baptism:</p>

<p><strong>Wisdom</strong> helps us see with God's eyes. <strong>Understanding</strong> helps us grasp the deep truths of faith. <strong>Counsel</strong> guides us in difficult decisions. <strong>Fortitude</strong> gives us strength to persevere. <strong>Knowledge</strong> helps us know God and ourselves. <strong>Piety</strong> draws us to worship. <strong>Fear of the Lord</strong> is reverence, not terror (CCC 1831).</p>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--brotes">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">Connection</h4>
<p>Read Isaiah 11:1-3, which foretells the Messiah bearing these gifts. Christ, the Anointed One, shares these gifts with us through Confirmation.</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Baptism and Confirmation: One Initiation</h2>

<p>
In the early Church, Baptism, Confirmation, and Eucharist were celebrated together as one initiation. Today, in the Eastern Churches, infants receive all three sacraments. In the Latin Church, Confirmation is often separated, which can make it seem like a "graduation" — but it is not. It completes what Baptism began (CCC 1289-1290).
</p>

<p>
The bishop is the ordinary minister of Confirmation because the sacrament expresses the unity of the whole Church. When the bishop confirms, he links us to the universal Church and to the apostolic succession (CCC 1313).
</p>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--raiz">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">Context</h4>
<p>Saint Thomas Aquinas taught that Confirmation gives spiritual courage for combat — not military combat, but spiritual battle against sin and evil. The confirmed are "soldiers of Christ," armed for the mission.</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Biblical Connection: Pentecost</h2>

<p>
The prototype of Confirmation is Pentecost. After the Ascension, the apostles gathered in prayer. Suddenly, "a sound like the rushing of a mighty wind" filled the house, and "tongues of fire" rested on each one. They were all filled with the Holy Spirit and began to speak in other languages (Acts 2:1-4).
</p>

<blockquote>
<p>"You will receive power when the Holy Spirit has come upon you, and you will be my witnesses."</p>
<cite>Acts 1:8</cite>
</blockquote>

<p>
That same Spirit is given in Confirmation. The same power that transformed fearful fishermen into bold martyrs is available to us.
</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>For Reflection</h2>

<ol>
<li>When were you confirmed? What did it mean to you at the time?</li>
<li>Which of the seven gifts do you most need right now in your life?</li>
<li>Where do you struggle to witness to your faith? How can Confirmation help?</li>
<li>Do you feel "sealed" as God's own? What would it mean to live that way?</li>
<li>How can you cooperate with the Holy Spirit's gifts this week?</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Key Idea</h2>

<div class="callout">
<p><strong>Key Idea:</strong> Confirmation seals us with the Holy Spirit, empowering us to live as witnesses of Christ in word and deed.</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Cultural Richness</h2>

<p>
<strong>Saint Cyril of Jerusalem (4th century)</strong> &mdash; In his Mystagogical Catecheses, Saint Cyril explained Confirmation to the newly baptized: "You were anointed with holy oil... and were sealed with the seal of the Spirit." His words shaped how the Church understands this sacrament.
</p>

<p>
<strong>The Chrism Mass</strong> &mdash; Each Holy Thursday, bishops consecrate the sacred chrism used for Confirmation throughout the year. The oil is blessed with ancient prayers, connecting each confirmation to the bishop and to the whole Church.
</p>

</section>

</LessonLayout>